13:T57a,On September 4, 2022, a significant incident occurred at the Hyundai-LG battery plant construction site, where several foreign workers, primarily from South Korea, were detained by U. S.  authorities.  The detentions were reportedly related to alleged violations of visa terms, though the specifics of these alleged violations were the subject of much debate and discussion both locally and internationally.  This incident quickly escalated into a diplomatic matter, as the South Korean government became actively involved in ensuring the workers’ safe return to their home country.  The South Korean Ministry of Foreign Affairs took immediate steps to coordinate with U. S.  authorities, emphasizing the importance of both the safe repatriation of their citizens and the prevention of any long-term restrictions or bans on the future admission of South Korean workers into the United States.  South Korea’s Foreign Minister, Cho Hyun, personally traveled to Washington, D. C. , to engage with U. S.  officials, highlighting the seriousness with which the South Korean government viewed the situation.  Minister Cho’s discussions included efforts to streamline and potentially reform the visa process, aiming to prevent similar incidents in the future and to ensure that South Korean workers could continue to contribute to large-scale infrastructure and industrial projects in the United States.4:[["$","$Lf",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"NewsArticle\",\"mainEntityOfPage\":{\"@type\":\"WebPage\",\"@id\":\"https://www.pressorahub.com/us/korean-workers-detained-georgia-raid-return-home-fear-lingers/\"},\"headline\":\"Plans in the Works for Korean Workers Detained in Raid to Go Home While Fear Lingers for Residents\",\"description\":\"Over 300 South Korean workers detained during a U.S. immigration raid in Georgia are set to return home, while local Korean residents express ongoing concerns about their safety and future in the community.\",\"image\":\"/images/korean-workers-detained-georgia-raid.webp\",\"author\":{\"@type\":\"Person\",\"name\":\"Lauren Jacobs\",\"url\":\"https://www.pressorahub.com/our-team/\"},\"publisher\":{\"@type\":\"Organization\",\"name\":\"PressoraHub\",\"logo\":{\"@type\":\"ImageObject\",\"url\":\"https://www.pressorahub.com/images/pressorahub-icon.webp\"}},\"datePublished\":\"2025-06-18T00:00:00.000Z\",\"dateModified\":\"2025-06-18T00:00:00.000Z\"}"}}],["$","$L10",null,{"breadcrumbTitle":"Plans in the Works for Korean Workers Detained in Raid to Go Home While Fear Lingers for Residents","category":"us","children":["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","section",null,{"className":"post-layout-1 padding","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"container","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"top-content","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"post-card single-card","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"post-content","children":[["$","$L11",null,{"title":"korean-workers-detained-georgia-raid-return-home-fear-lingers","href":"/us","className":"card-btn bg-green","children":"us"}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"title","style":{"lineHeight":1.3},"children":"Plans in the Works for Korean Workers Detained in Raid to Go Home While Fear Lingers for Residents"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"post-list","children":[["$","li",null,{"className":"author","children":["by ",["$","span",null,{"children":"Lauren Jacobs"}]]}],["$","li",null,{"className":"date","children":"October 13, 2025"}]]}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Over 300 South Korean workers detained during a U.S. immigration raid in Georgia are set to return home, while local Korean residents express ongoing concerns about their safety and future in the community."}]]}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"post-social","children":["$","ul",null,{"className":"social-list d-flex align-items-center","children":[["$","li",null,{"children":["$","$L11",null,{"href":"/#","title":"facebook","classNa